Z||000009||88formula||07-22-2001||02:35 AM||||<P>Connecting rod bolts ARP-133-6002 <BR>Head stud kit ARP-233-4003<BR>Main stud s you have to order directly from ARP because there is no kit. I got the following  <BR>8 - Studs (11mm X 1.5 & 7/16" - 20) - ATP4.5L <BR>8 - Nuts (7/16" - 20 Hex Nut) - APN58 <BR>8 - Washers (7/16 ID 13/16 OD) - APW1316N when you order the ARP fasteners don’t forget the molly lube. Get a bolt stretch gauge if you can afford it, otherwise follow the ARP torque recommendations not GM recommendations.<BR>I got the ROL gasket set FS32646 but the Fel pro is an excellent choice too. The parts person should be able to help you with that. Make sure you get the rubber valve cover and oil pan gaskets, some Felpro and lesser gasket brands don’t come with rubber gaskets, they come with cork instead.  <P>Get the SI valves; I’ll e-mail you that information. <BR>Get a decent set of molly rings. There is no such thing as Chrome Molly rings! I think mine were speed-pro or Federal Mogul, same thing. Stay away from those gapless rings, they are a scam and a waste of money. <P>I’m tired and it’s late so I may forget some things but here is what I did to my 3.4. These are my recommendations.  <P>For the heads I recommend you do what I did. If you are on a tight budget than spend your money on the heads. I fitted them with SI stainless steel valves, ported/polished them myself and had a performance valve job done on them. The exhaust ports are so polished you can see your reflection in them. The intake ports I basically just cleaned up the flash and smoothed out the bowl area. I didn’t make the ports bigger in fear of loosing low-end torque and I left the hump in the intake runner completely alone. I had the machinist back cut the valves just above the point where the valve meets the seat for more low lift flow, a trick I learned from the Chevy Power book. The intake seats have three angles on them and the exhaust seats have a radius on them. The machinist used carbide cutters not stones. The heads are fitted with GM springs and retainers. The springs are set to the correct installed height too. <P>Bore the block out as little as you can. If you think boring your motor out to .060” will make a big difference it won’t. It may make less power because the cylinder walls will flex and distort more causing the rings to be less effective. If you can bore it .020” do it. Get the hypereutectic pistons not the regular cast pistons. Hypereutectic pistons are cast but they have more silicone in them making them stronger and more scuff resistant. Silvolite pistons are an excellent choice. TRW sells forged pistons that are supposed to give you a 9.5-1 compression ration. With a higher compression ratio your engine will make more power and get better gas mileage but you may have to use higher octane fuel.  <P>Have the machinist balance everything and I mean everything. I balanced the pistons, rods, crank, damper, flywheel, pressure plate, and pistons with the rings. All of these things where out of balance in my buildup. My engine is super smooth and revs very quickly and smoothly, much more smoothly than my 2.8 ever did. <P>Get a new damper if your old one has any cracks in the rubber, mine did and it only had 50k on it. I got a new one for $46 from GM performance parts. I them had to use a dial indicator to locate TDC on the motor and mark the damper. The mark GM cut was off quit a bit, the machinist said GM dampers are always off. I then cut a new mark in the new damper with a hacksaw to locate TDC and avoid any timing nightmares in the future. Don’t try to transfer the mark from your old damper onto the new one because this one may be off too, my damper was off. <P>You don’t have to do this especially if your on a budget but I decked the block to get the quench area down to .050”. I was aiming for .040” but do to an idiot machinist (long story) I got a little more. Do this modification if you use high compression pistons. There is a great description of this modification on Silvolites website. You will have to have your lower intake manifold planed if you do this. Save all your old gaskets. I used an old intake gasket and torqued the manifold down with it in place to see if the manifold lined up. I then measured the gasket and had the machinist cut the manifold that much, .035” for me. <P>Get a decent exhaust system with mandrel bends, leave no bottlenecks where the exhaust is restricted. An engine looses up to 40% of the power produced from the gasoline to pump the gases, if you make it easier for the engine to get the spent gases out you will cut down your loses. The headers really help too. <P>I got an aluminum flywheel, weighs only 7.8 lbs. compared to the stock 16 lbs. (Fiero Shop). It will hurt your launch a little bit but your engine will rocket out of corners and tach up quickly like it is on steroids, sounds really cool when you rev your engine and it rockets from idle to 4+ grand in a split second. Since I have a 3.4-liter engine it has more than plenty of low-end grunt to help my car launch, maybe too much torque. Either way you can’t loose with this modification.  <P>I polished the throttle body and gasket matched the throttle body to the intake, using Dyno2000 I could see no real benefits to boring it out a little bigger for $150. When you set your valve lash use a dial indicator to set it. I positioned it over the lifter and turned the rocker nut until I got .030” pre-load. The nut turned exactly 180-degrees for mine so I then set the rest of then by turning the nut a half a turn. I have Crane roller rockers and the threads are different than stock so use a dial indicator to check it for yourself, You want the plunger to travel .030” down the lifter. <P>Crankshaft, you don’t need a forged crankshaft, in fact I have never seen one available for the 60-degreee-v6 engine. The stock crankshaft is quit strong. The problem is the oiling to the connecting rods not the strength of the crankshaft. If I where you I would get the “How to rebuild your 60-degree v6 engine book” and the “Chevy Power” book and follow the recommendations they give for oiling. These are a must if you want to learn how to build this engine right. What you want to look out for is the fillets on the rod and main journals. If the crankshaft is turned too much you loose these fillets and the crankshaft looses its strength. Turn your crankshaft down as little as possible. If you have to turn it down more than .010” I would just get a replacement. Try to get an internally balanced crankshaft if you can, they are better. <P>Get a high volume high quality oil pump like GM or Melling. Don’t go cheap here. Increasing the oil pressure is unnecessary unless you’re going to rev your engine past 6-6.5k. <P>My biggest mistake I maid was going with the cheapest machinist I could find in the beginning. That cost me big (long story) and I learned a hard lesson. I also lost a lot of money and had to toss some of my parts in the trash. Go with a machinist that has a reputation for quality work and pay him what he is worth. If you don’t listen to me here and go with the cheap guy you will be sorry. Talk to the people in your area. Go to some speed shops and ask who they use. Go by word of mouth not your intuition no matter how good you think yours is. Good machine work is more important than what kind of parts you use. Finding a good machinist is like an art. Preferably use a machinist who builds performance race engines for a living. <P>Degree your camshaft, mine was our 3-degrees. Check your piston to valve clearance. Keep everything super clean. <P>Here is what I get from Dyno2000 for your engine buildup. With 8.9 compression, stock heads, ported manifolds with high flow exhaust, and Comp Cams H-252 camshaft I get 148 horsepower from 4500-5000 rpm and 191-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. If you have ported heads you will have 154 horsepower @ 5000 RPM and 193-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. <P>With 9.5 compression and ported heads the power output jumps to 160 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 198-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. The power output jumps up across the whole rpm band or course, peak numbers only tell part of the story. <P>For a baseline I get 137 horsepower from 4500-5000 rpm and 175-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm for a stock GM 2.8-liter engine in a fiero. <P>  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||07-22-2001||07:32 AM||||Thanks for the write-up 88formula. I wasn't expecting that much information, but its very helpful.<P>The silv-o-lites on my list are hyper. It took quite a bit of digging because on KBs website, the part# refers to cast, not hyper. I looked into forged pistons, but they are not what I am looking for, even if price were no object. KB(UE-M) website has good info on them though. <P>I hadn't decided on balancing yet. Is it worth the added cost? What is the typical cost for that job? I'll check with the machinest too, be he isn't in on Sunday. I don't plan on hammering on this engine, but I can see some(possibly high-speed) cross country trips in the future <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Melling high-volume is already on the list, #M-95HV.<P>Piston to valve clearance is my biggest concern right now. The pistons are destroked to allow for maching(#1463, 8.5c/r) but I worry about that cam punching valves into pistons still.<P>ARP fasterners still have me a little concerned since they don't strech like tpyical bolts. And I've never heard of the stock bolts letting go, I might still go with a new set of factory hardened bolts. Any thoughts there?<P>I've got several books here, including the gm v-6 60* book. I was looking for more SBC books since they typically have a lot of good information that is "transferrable."<P>I have an '86 crank at the moment from the '86 block I am using, but I will be putting an '88 crank in. AutoZone carries 2 crank part #s. I stopped by yesterday to ask how much is taken off by the "rebuilder." One part # doesn't say(I said I didn't want it then), and the other was guaranteed .010" over, but of course, the supplier is out of them for the time being. Pretty hard time finding a neutral crank in the yards, and the only yard was Riverside, the biggest rip-job-scam I've ever seen. $250 plus a core, plus it needs to be turned still. WTF.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe Torma||07-22-2001||11:41 AM||||I agree with everything that 88' has said.  Except for the dyno #s.  88...are you using Seat to Seat or @.05.  What is the cam spec'd at?  Also, I think the duration stated was incorrect.  I'm pretty sure should be 252/260 and that's what I used for my numbers.<P>Joe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||07-22-2001||11:54 AM||||Wow, it was only 5pm and I couldn't even read my paper correctly then.<P>Duration was posted incorrectly, here are correct numbers:<BR>advertised:<BR>int/exh- 252<BR>at .050" lift:<BR>int/exh- 206<P>everything else I posted should be correct.||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||07-22-2001||12:50 PM|||| You can get away with not balancing your motor but balancing the engine is one of those things that sets a regular old cheap rebuild apart from a professional high performance rebuild. I rebuilt two engines where I didn't balance the reciprocating assembles and they are still running but I swear one of them, an odd fire 90-degree v6, doesn't run as smoothly as it did before. Of course no odd fire v6 ever runs smoothly. The rotating parts can be compared to a washing machine with all of the clothes on one side. The result is vibration and worn parts. <P>Just to give you an idea of what balancing does for an engine I got this statistic from my engine building book. “An imbalance of ¼ ounce (7 grams or about one sheet of paper) at a 4” radius on a rotating part creates forces of” <P>7 lbs. @ 2000 rpm<BR>28 lbs. @ 4000 rpm<BR>63 lbs. @ 6000 rpm<BR>112 lbs. @ 8000 rpm. <P>I know the 2.8-liter engine has a 3” radius but the point is I think the importance of balancing is a must. All my parts were out of balance except for the pistons, which to my surprise were OK. My first set of pistons was not. My rods where off, my flywheel was way off, my new GM damper was off, my pressure plate was off a little, and the list goes on. Right now my engine is the smoothest most rev happy engine I have driven. I paid $175 but it ranges anywhere from $150-$375. It usually depends on how bad it was off. The place I took my engine too charges by the hour.<P>“Is it worth the added cost”, in my opinion you can’t afford not to do it, but everyone has a different opinion on this subject and they will tell you differently. I choose to do it and I’m glade I did. I think it was the best $175 I spent on that rebuild. <P>The camshaft you have is relatively mild and doesn’t have much more lift than stock. I wouldn’t worry about the piston to valve clearance too much but I would definitely still check it. If you have never checked piston to valve clearance than go to Cranes wed page. They have a great how to section that is informative and tells you how to check this. <P>Every serious high performance engine builder uses APR fasteners. I have never heard of anyone using ARP fasteners where the fasteners failed or got loosened. You probably haven’t heard of anyone who had a stock fastener fail because, their engines are stock. I had a fastener fail on one of my iron duke motors. A head bolt snapped. With a high performance engine the stock fasteners will be stressed more than they are in a stock engine and there is more of a chance that the fastener will fail. GM fasteners are cheap junk and you have to replace the heads bolts anyway, so why not just spend the extra money for the good stuff? APR fasteners are designed to stretch. This is where a fastener’s claming force comes from. If you look at their short fasteners and then their longer ones side by side you will notice that the shorter fastener is necked down. This is to allow the fastener to stretch since shorter fasteners tend to stretch less than longer one’s. In fact the correct way to tighten an ARP fastener is to use a stretch gauge!!!  If you have any concerns about this I would call ARP directly and talk to one of their tech people. They will be more than helpful.<P>Get this book, its got tons of high performance information for all of GM’s engines. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Chevy_Power_book..jpg">  <P>One more thing about the crankshaft, if you can get one turned yourself than have the machinist correct the stoke from one journal to the other. They are usually off and remanufactured crankshafts are usually not corrected. <P>The camshaft specifications that I used are lift .425/.425, duration seat to seat 252/252, and valve opening and closing points are intake open/close 20/52 exhaust open/close 60/12. I have over 200 v6 cam specifications so you only need to tell me what camshaft you have and I can find the rest. <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-22-2001).]||||reg||1
