Z||000016||Shiner||07-22-2001||02:40 PM||||It might just be me, but it seems silly to put all that time and money into an engine swap and come out with only 160hp.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||07-22-2001||03:52 PM||||I agree, $1500 for an engine swap and only getting 160hp would be rediculous. But, I'm not performing an engine swap. I'm performing an engine <B>rebuild</B>. The difference is I'm using the same engine, only this time, it won't knock. And since in my research, I found the factory cam has a 100K mile designed life span, putting another 170K on it might be pushing it. Factor in everything else is usually worn(entire valve-train, timing chain, seals, bearings, etc)I don't think $1500 seems all that bad. A Grooms reman 2.8 I think is around $1600(I never looked into it), and that doesn't include all new parts. I think they only replace the pistons, bearings, timing chain, and rings(maybe more or less)most everything else is original to the engine. <P>My Formula is to me, like the Civic is to the rest of the world. It is my primary transportation. I don't need a dragger, I need good mileage that can get me around, and I don't want a duke unless it is a red '84 automatic(I love those '84s, everyone of them).||||reg||1
Z||000018||Shiner||07-22-2001||05:15 PM||||56,<P> I wasn't talking about your rebuild.  $1500 including machine work is very reasonable.  But putting in a 3.4 really is a swap.||||reg||1
